  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a hard drive platter?",
      "a": "A platter is a perfectly smooth disc of aluminium or glass coated with a thin magnetic layer. Data is written in rings called tracks, and a drive may stack several platters on one spindle.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the disc inside a hard drive?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How fast does a hard drive spin?",
      "a": "Most hard drives spin at 5,400 or 7,200 revolutions per minute, and some server drives at 10,000 or 15,000. At 7,200 rpm the platter turns 120 times every second.",
      "asks": [
        "What does 7200 rpm mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the read/write head write data?",
      "a": "The head is a tiny electromagnet. Current through it makes a magnetic field that sets the direction of the patch beneath it, north one way for a 1 and the other way for a 0. To read, a sensor in the head feels those directions as it passes.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a hard drive read data?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the arm find the right place?",
      "a": "A coil on the arm sits between strong magnets, like a loudspeaker. Current in the coil swings the arm, and servo markings on the platter tell the drive exactly which track the head is over.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the actuator arm?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do hard drives break if you drop them?",
      "a": "The head flies only a few nanometres above the spinning platter. A jolt can slam it into the surface, a head crash, scratching off the magnetic coating and the data with it. Many drives park the head when they sense a fall.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a head crash?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "IBM's first hard drive, the RAMAC 350 of 1956, stored about 5 megabytes on fifty 24-inch platters.",
    "Modern hard drive heads fly only a few nanometres above the platter surface."
  ],
